From: Ren <ren@localhost>
Subject: [ren-fork] Render export batches in ascending frame order

Upstream file: crates/export/src/job.rs
Upstream revision: 0800a8b0aa4a703ba42172f415b45a61f8d8a5e1 (FilmCraft CLI 0.6.0)
Ren lane: vep-mxf-ingest-profile (2026-10-11); re-based onto 0800a8b by
vep-mxf-decode-patch (2026-10-11, hunks identical, apply-check clean on both pins)

Rationale
---------

GOP-aware video sources (MXF Long-GOP via GopCache) restart their decoder at
the preceding sync sample on any out-of-order frame request, then decode
forward to the wanted frame. Reel 22 media index tables flag one sync
sample per 14,400 frames (measured key_positions on both A-roll files:
LCATL0013.MXF 7 keyframes / 98,292 samples; LC4932.MXF 9 / 118,468), while
the stream itself carries I-frames roughly every 12 frames (ffprobe) - so
every restart re-decodes up to a ~14k-frame preroll that a true-sync seek
would not need (follow-up lead, not this patch).

`render_batch` dispatched each batch's frames over rayon (`into_par_iter`),
so every batch issued out-of-order requests against one shared running
decoder. Measured on the pinned revision (perf.stats diffs around
file.exportMedia, same machine, read-only sources):

- 24 frames placed at source frame 87030 (630 frames past flagged sync
  86400): 10 seeks, 6,463 samples decoded for 24 requested (269x
  amplification), render stage 157.7 s of a 158.6 s export (99.4%).
- 120 frames placed at source frame 0: 10 seeks, 274 samples for 120
  requested (2.3x), render stage 23.9 s of a 29.1 s export.

Rendering the batch sequentially in ascending order keeps one running
decoder per source: ~1 seek per flagged-sync entry plus the wanted frames
(~674 samples for the deep case above, ~9.6x fewer decodes).

What changes: request order only. Batches are still cut exactly as before,
frames are still encoded in order, and the batch-level render/encode overlap
(`overlapping`, `in_place_scope`) is untouched - so the output bytes are
unchanged (deterministic per-frame render; `determinism_tests` still apply).
No pixel or container change: fidelity-neutral by construction; the standard
intake corpus re-verifies at promotion.

Expected saving (bounded from the measured counts x measured per-sample
CPU: 66 ms shallow / 215 ms deep thread-time over 10 h264 threads):
deep-24 export render stage ~158 s -> ~20 s (~8x: 674 x 215 ms / 10 plus
unchanged per-frame overhead and encode). Shallow-120 is NOT decode-bound
(decode thread-time 18.2 s is ~8% of the 23.9 s render wall; the rest is
per-frame pipeline overhead this patch does not touch), so its bounded
saving is only the 154 excess samples (~1 s wall) plus whatever lock
contention the parallel requests were adding - re-measure at promotion,
do not quote ~1.7x. Full-reel effect scales with the restart rate of
longer exports; the bound is per restart avoided, not a whole-reel
re-measurement.

Remove this patch when upstream orders batch frame requests (or otherwise
avoids GOP restarts from parallel batch rendering); re-measure
samplesDecoded/requests at each pin move.

